  "headline": "CBA's return on equity (cash basis, continuing operations) rose 130 basis points, from 10.2% in FY20 (restated) to 11.5% in FY21. The bank attributes the move to the impact of higher profit (approximately 200 basis points), partly offset by an increase in capital levels (approximately 70 basis points). Derived per the constant-equity identity: earnings_effect = 10.2% x 19.8% cash NPAT growth = +2.02ppt; equity_effect = 1.3 - 2.02 = -0.72ppt, as average equity rose 6.1% to $75,187m. The profit lift came from loan impairment expense down $1,964m to $554m (-78%) on improved economic conditions and outlook, operating income +1.7% (NII +1.2% on 3.6%/$32bn average interest earning asset growth, NIM -4bps) and expenses +3.3%. Equity grew because ~29% of the $8,653m cash profit was retained at an unchanged 71% payout and CET1 rose 150bps to 13.1%; the $6.0bn off-market buy-back was announced with FY21 results and executed after year-end. Statutory-basis ROE rose 140bps (11.8% vs 10.4%); including discontinued operations, +120bps (11.7% vs 10.5%).",
